WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.297
Searched CASE is the flexible form of CASE because every WHEN contains a complete Boolean condition.

00:00:06.297 --> 00:00:13.747
That makes it ideal for ranges, multiple-field rules, NULL checks, and complicated classifications.

00:00:13.747 --> 00:00:17.502
In the billing example, we handle NULL first.

00:00:17.502 --> 00:00:23.357
That is important because a missing bill should not quietly fall into the Low-Value category.

00:00:23.357 --> 00:00:27.877
Then we check the highest threshold, followed by the next threshold,

00:00:27.877 --> 00:00:31.442
and finally use ELSE for the remaining known amounts.

00:00:31.442 --> 00:00:37.312
Read the logic like a decision tree: if the bill is missing, label it Unknown.

00:00:37.312 --> 00:00:42.797
Otherwise, if it is at least the high-value threshold, label it High-Value.

00:00:42.797 --> 00:00:47.982
Otherwise, if it is at least the standard threshold, label it Standard.

00:00:47.982 --> 00:00:49.237
Otherwise, label it Low-Value.

00:00:49.237 --> 00:00:56.272
This same structure can classify students by risk level, customers by spending tier, patients by severity,

00:00:56.272 --> 00:00:57.957
or servers by CPU utilization.

00:00:57.957 --> 00:01:02.672
When ranges are involved, pay attention to exact boundaries.

00:01:02.672 --> 00:01:10.257
If High-Value starts at ten thousand, then exactly ten thousand belongs there.

00:01:10.257 --> 00:01:15.847
If Standard starts at five thousand, then exactly five thousand belongs in Standard.

00:01:15.847 --> 00:01:20.202
Run the query and inspect NULL, zero, and threshold values.

00:01:20.202 --> 00:01:25.431
Boundary testing is how we confirm that the written rule and the SQL rule are the same.
